.SS "int32_t schultz_image_load_file (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, const char * path, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_image)"

.PP
Loads an image from a file, deciding the format from its contents\&. PNG, JPEG, WebP and SVG all load through here\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to load into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpath\fP Path to the file\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_image\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the file is missing, unreadable, or holds nothing this build can decode, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_load_data (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, const void * bytes, uint32_t size, const char * kind, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_image)"

.PP
Loads an image already in memory\&. The bytes are copied, so the caller's buffer need not outlive the call\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to load into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The encoded image\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIsize\fP How many bytes\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIkind\fP A hint at the format, such as 'png' or 'svg', or NULL to let the decoder work it out from the bytes\&. 
.br
\fIout_image\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the bytes could not be decoded, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_set_pixels (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, const uint32_t * pixels, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t stride, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_image)"

.PP
Takes raw pixels, already decoded by the application\&. This is the path for everything Schultz does not decode: a video frame, an animated GIF the application unpacked itself, a picture generated in code\&. Pixels are premultiplied ARGB8888, the same layout the window buffer uses, and are copied\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to load into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpixels\fP The pixels, row major\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP Width in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP Height in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIstride\fP Pixels per row, which may exceed the width\&. Zero means the rows are packed\&. 
.br
\fIout_image\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_encode (const uint32_t * pixels, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t stride, uint32_t format, const void ** out_bytes, uint64_t * out_length)"

.PP
Writes pixels out as a file format, in memory\&. The other direction from loading\&. What comes back is the bytes of a whole file, ready to be put on a clipboard, sent somewhere or written to disk\&.
.PP
Pixels go in premultiplied, the way everything else in the toolkit holds them, and come out straight, because that is what a file format means by an alpha channel\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpixels\fP The pixels, row major, premultiplied ARGB8888\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP Width in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP Height in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIstride\fP Pixels per row, which may exceed the width\&. Zero means the rows are packed\&. 
.br
\fIformat\fP SCHULTZ_IMAGE_PNG or SCHULTZ_IMAGE_BMP\&. 
.br
\fIout_bytes\fP Receives the file's bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. Owned by the toolkit and valid until the next call to this function, so a caller wanting two formats at once copies the first\&. 
.br
\fIout_length\fP Receives how many bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_load_animation (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, const char * path, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_image)"

.PP
Loads a Lottie animation from a file\&. Lottie is the animation format the toolkit reads, and it is a good one: vector, small, and with the frame handling already compiled in\&. There is no GIF and no video; both take the raw pixel path instead\&.
.PP
The animation loads showing its first frame and stays there\&. Something has to move it, which is what schultz_lottie_create does with the tree's clock, or an application can step it by hand with schultz_image_set_frame\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to load into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpath\fP Path to the Lottie JSON\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_image\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when it could not be read or decoded, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_frames (const \fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP image, float * out_frames, float * out_duration)"

.PP
Reads how long an animation runs\&. A still picture reports zero for both, which is how one is told apart from an animation\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table holding it\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIimage\fP A handle from one of the load calls\&. 
.br
\fIout_frames\fP Receives the frame count, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.br
\fIout_duration\fP Receives the length in seconds, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_set_frame (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP image, float frame)"

.PP
Moves an animation to a frame\&. Frames are counted from zero and clamped to what there is\&. Fractions are allowed and are what makes playback smooth rather than stepped\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table holding it\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIimage\fP A handle from schultz_image_load_animation\&. 
.br
\fIframe\fP Which frame to show\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when the image is a still picture, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_size (const \fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP image, \fBschultz_size\fP * out_size)"

.PP
Reads an image's natural size\&. This is what an Icon measures to when it is not given a size of its own\&. A vector image reports the size its author drew it at, and scales cleanly from there\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table holding it\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIimage\fP A handle from one of the load calls\&. 
.br
\fIout_size\fP Receives the size in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_image_unload (\fBschultz_image_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP image)"

.PP
Forgets one image and releases what it decoded to\&. 
